### Suggested solutions

From https://travis-ci.com/Qiskit/qiskit-terra/jobs/220988466#L14523

```
OPENQASM 2.0;
include "qelib1.inc";
qreg q9298[1];
qreg q9299[1];
creg c1352[1];
h q9298[0];
h q9298[0];
h q9298[0];
h q9299[0];
cu3(0.0,0.0,2044.54406738108) q9299[0],q9298[0];
h q9299[0];
measure q9299[0] -> c1352[0];
```
raises, for optimization_level 3
```
qiskit.exceptions.QiskitError: 'TwoQubitWeylDecomposition: failed to diagonalize M2'
```

#2800 bound the magnitude of gate angles in randomized testing to \pm 1e10, but maybe that wasn't enough. Maybe we can try some small-integer multiple of pi to see if we generate some more interesting failures.

There have been a number of attempts at fixing barriers in the latex drawer (https://github.com/Qiskit/qiskit-terra/pull/1727, https://github.com/Qiskit/qiskit-terra/pull/1534, https://github.com/Qiskit/qiskit-terra/pull/1361), but there are still bugs.

```python
from qiskit import *
qasm = """
OPENQASM 2.0;
include "qelib1.inc";
qreg q[7];
cx q[1],q[6];
barrier q[1],q[4],q[5],q[6];
u3(1.8,-0.5,0.4) q[1];
cx q[0],q[1];
"""
circ = QuantumCircuit.from_qasm_str(qasm)
circ.draw(output='latex')
```
![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/8622381/62546337-4b6f3b80-b831-11e9-8595-a2aadb532a84.png)


Bug 1: The barrier should only be on qubits 1, 4, 5, 6 -- but it is on qubits 1 to 4.

Bug 2: It cuts through the U3 gate.

```python
qasm = """OPENQASM 2.0;
include "qelib1.inc";
qreg q[5];
creg c[5];
cx q[4],q[1];
u3(0.771421551875327*pi,1.93195729272833*pi,1.95526496079462*pi) q[2];
cx q[1],q[4];
cx q[3],q[2];
cx q[4],q[1];
u1(0.0680407993035097*pi) q[2];
u3(0.474906730130952*pi,0.679527728632899*pi,1.16361128456302*pi) q[1];
cx q[2],q[3];
cx q[1],q[0];
u3(0.479236620087447*pi,0,0) q[3];
u3(1.65884252662582*pi,0,0) q[1];
cx q[3],q[2];
cx q[0],q[1];
cx q[2],q[0];
cx q[1],q[0];
cx q[3],q[2];
cx q[0],q[1];
cx q[2],q[3];
cx q[1],q[0];
cx q[3],q[2];
cx q[0],q[4];
cx q[1],q[2];
measure q[4] -> c[4];
measure q[0] -> c[0];
measure q[1] -> c[1];
measure q[2] -> c[2];
measure q[3] -> c[3];
"""

from qiskit import QuantumCircuit, execute, transpile, assemble, Aer
from qiskit.transpiler import CouplingMap
from qiskit.visualization import plot_histogram
from qiskit.transpiler import passes
from qiskit.transpiler import PassManager

circ = QuantumCircuit().from_qasm_str(qasm)
backend = Aer.get_backend('qasm_simulator')
coupling_map = [[0, 1], [1, 0], [1, 2], [1, 3], [2, 1], [3, 1], [3, 4], [4, 3]]

stochastic = passes.StochasticSwap(CouplingMap(coupling_map), seed=0)
lookahead = passes.LookaheadSwap(CouplingMap(coupling_map))
basic = passes.BasicSwap(CouplingMap(coupling_map))

pm = PassManager(stochastic)
new_circ = pm.run(circ)

counts_cm = backend.run(assemble(new_circ,shots=10000)).result().get_counts()
counts_no_cm = backend.run(assemble(circ,shots=10000)).result().get_counts()

plot_histogram([counts_cm,counts_no_cm],legend=['coupling_map','no_coupling_map'])
```

![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/8622381/66239363-abfee580-e6c7-11e9-804e-11f3f37b7447.png)


The circuit mapped to coupling map returns different counts vs. original circuit.

This only occurs for some random seeds.

_Originally posted by @1ucian0 in https://github.com/Qiskit/qiskit-terra/pull/3393#issuecomment-592720851_

> > 3. Can we write the loaded QASM back as the same QASM (with the custom definitions)?
> 
> You mean `circuit.qasm()`? Let's leave the exporter out of this PR for now...
> 
> As a reference, this is the exporter behavior:
> ```
> qasm_string = """OPENQASM 2.0;
>                   include "qelib1.inc";
>                   gate my_other_gate(phi,lambda) q
>                     {u3(asin(cos(phi)/2), phi+pi, lambda/2) q;}
>                   gate my_gate(phi) r
>                     {my_other_gate(phi, phi+pi) r;}
>                   qreg qr[1];
>                   my_gate(pi) qr[0];"""
> circuit = QuantumCircuit.from_qasm_str(qasm_string)
> print(circuit.qasm())
> ```
> ```
> OPENQASM 2.0;
> include "qelib1.inc";
> qreg qr[1];
> my_gate(3.141592653589793) qr[0]; 
> ```

e.g.

```c
OPENQASM 2.0;
include "qelib1.inc";
qreg q[4];
creg meas[4];
delay(172) q[1];
u3(1.955526,-3.9994262,1.5396532) q[1];
```

### Environment

- **Qiskit Terra version**: 0.19.dev @ 2c402dd5c
- **Python version**: any
- **Operating system**: any

### What is happening?

The QASM3 exporter (and the QASM2 exporter) does not handle arbitrary parameterised gates correctly.  It emits a gate definition which includes parameters in the signature, but hardcodes them in the body to the first instance of the gate it found.  This is because there's currently no way in Terra to get from a gate with its parameters assigned back to the fully parameterised version.

### How can we reproduce the issue?

```python
In [1]: import qiskit.qasm3
   ...: qc = qiskit.QuantumCircuit(2, 2)
   ...: qc.rzx(0.2, 0, 1)
   ...: qc.rzx(0.3, 0, 1)
   ...:
   ...: print(qiskit.qasm3.dumps(qc))
OPENQASM 3;
include "stdgates.inc";
gate rzx(_gate_p_0) _gate_q_0, _gate_q_1 {
  h _gate_q_1;
  cx _gate_q_0, _gate_q_1;
  rz(0.2) _gate_q_1;
  cx _gate_q_0, _gate_q_1;
  h _gate_q_1;
}
bit[2] c;
qubit[2] _all_qubits;
let q = _all_qubits[0:1];
rzx(0.2) q[0], q[1];
rzx(0.3) q[0], q[1];
```

The two `rzx` calls have different parameters, but the parameter is hardcoded to 0.2 in the actual definition.

### What should happen?

A full fix of this issue will have the `rz` line in the `rzx` gate definition use the gate parameter.

### Any suggestions?

As a temporary measure before the 0.19 release, we can hack the exporter to always output a new declaration for each instance of every gate that is not in the include files.  This is pretty awful, and the output QASM won't correctly represent the structure of the program with re-used gates, but it will at least produce the correct behaviour.

### Environment

- **Qiskit Terra version**: 0.19.2
